.TH EUCLID-CLI-EMO-LIST-METRICS 1 "October 2026" "euclid-cli 2.1.3" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-emo-list-metrics \- list every metric Euclid monitoring has recorded, by name
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli emo list-metrics
[\fB\-\-prefix\fR|\fB\-p\fR \fIPREFIX\fR]
[\fB\-\-hours\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-names\-only\fR|\fB\-n\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Lists every metric with a five-minute row in the last
.I N
hours, sorted by name. For each: whether it is a
.I GAUGE
(averaged over a period) or a
.I RATE
(summed over it), how many series it has, the label names that tell
those series apart, and when it last reported.
.PP
The names are what
.B \-\-metric
takes in
.BR euclid-cli-emo-create-alert-rule (1),
and the label names what its
.B \-\-label
selects on.
.PP
There is no separate list of metrics anywhere: a metric exists in EMO
because something pushed it, and its rows are the only record of that.
So the window matters. The default of 24 hours includes metrics written
once an hour \- disk usage \- and leaves out one that stopped reporting a
week ago.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-prefix " " \fIPREFIX\fR
Only metrics whose name starts with this, e.g.
.IR system- .
.TP
.BR \-\-hours " " \fIN\fR
Only metrics with data in the last
.I N
hours. Default 24. Rows are kept for 7 days, so more than 168 adds
nothing.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-names\-only
Print the names alone, one per line, instead of JSON.
.SH OUTPUT
.RS
.nf
{"items": [{"name": "system-cpu-usage", "type": "GAUGE", "series": 3,
            "labelNames": ["host"], "lastSeen": "2026-10-31T11:40:00Z"}, ...],
 "total": 42}
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The metrics were printed.
.TP
.B 1
The caller was not authenticated, or is not an administrator.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
# Just the names
euclid-cli emo list-metrics --names-only

# The host metrics, with their series and labels
euclid-cli emo list-metrics --prefix system-

# Everything that reported in the last week
euclid-cli emo list-metrics --hours 168
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-emo (1),
.BR euclid-cli-emo-create-alert-rule (1)
